[llm-docs
  [doc [#
docs-src/llm/manifest.cxd — the LLM onboarding layer's document set (#938).

Read by scripts/gen_docs/primer_build.cx (`make docs`). Declares:

  [site]     the published base URL every llms.txt link is written against
  [tagline]  the one-line `>` blurb at the top of llms.txt
  [suites]   the conformance suites the templates may cite, one per line,
             relative to conformance/ and without the .cxd extension — or,
             for a module extracted into its own repository, its PINNED
             path `deps/<repo>/conformance/…` (RULED: RS-7, RS-12), the
             checkout the build embeds the module from.
             Declaring them bounds the fixture index (code.cxd alone is
             ~1200 cases) and records the primer's provenance in one place.
  [doc]      this block — ignored by the generator.
  [output]   one output file per entry, in llms-full.txt concatenation
             order. `section=` files the entry under one of llms.txt's
             headings — `start` | `build` | `reference` (default
             `reference`). The vocabulary is CLOSED: an unknown `section=`
             is a hard failure, because llms.txt IS the discovery surface
             and a document missing from it is a document nobody loads.

Adding a section that cites a new suite = add the suite here. A template
citing an undeclared (or nonexistent) fixture id is a HARD generator
failure, never a silently empty block.

CAPABILITIES — the non-obvious one. The generator replays each cited case
with EXACTLY the flags the case's own `grant=` attribute names (see
`argv-tokens` in primer_build.cx), and with none if it declares none. The
stdlib EVAL lane is more generous: it hands a full capability set to any
case whose `out-err` does not mention CXER0271. So a fixture that needs a
capability but declares no `grant=` is GREEN in the gate and UNCITABLE
here — `make docs` fails it as EXAMPLE FRESHNESS with a CXER0271 as the
live output. Seen 2026-09-08 on the three xap-dist publish/pin cases,
which mint an ed25519 keypair (`crypto`/`did`/`store`) and declare
nothing. Cite such a case by id in prose, or add the `grant=` it actually
needs to the fixture — but note that adding `grant=` also narrows how the
eval lane runs it, from the full set to exactly that least-privilege list,
so it is a change to that suite's contract and not a docs-only edit.

RETIRING a document means deleting its file in the SAME change that drops
its [output] entry: `--check` fails on any file under docs/llm/ that no
entry claims (an ORPHAN), because a file the manifest does not know about
is one the freshness contract cannot reach.
#]]

  [; `base` addresses the reference set; `root` is the SITE ROOT, where the
     two llmstxt.org files live (the convention is /llms.txt — a copy under
     /llm/ is not discoverable and does not count).

     WHO PUTS THEM AT THE ROOT IS AN OPEN CALL. scripts/publish.sh did, and it
     retired with the allowlist mirror (RULED: RS-11): there is no second tree
     to copy into any more, and this repository's own docs/ is the site. The
     three answers -- generate the pair into docs/ as well (a 360 KB duplicate
     of llms-full.txt in every docs regeneration), MOVE it there (AGENTS.md is
     Tier-1 and links docs/llm/llms.txt, and tools/release-verify.sh has a row
     on the path), or move `root=` to the /llm/ subdirectory and give up the
     llmstxt.org convention -- are the front-door branch's LETTER 2, in
     _gate_evidence/pipeline_frontdoor/RESULTS.md. Until it is answered the
     generator writes them where it always did. ]
  [site base='https://cxhome.org/llm/' root='https://cxhome.org/']

  [tagline [#
CX is a homoiconic data-and-code language: one bracketed element syntax for
documents, queries, programs and its own AST. It post-dates every model's
training data, so this layer is the context an assistant needs to write it
correctly.
#]]

  [; #1427-c put a module's corpus in its RING's directory, so a suite is
     named by the directory it is in: `stdlib/` is Ring 1, `platform/` is
     in the platform group, `x/` and `xap/` are the tiers the ring split does not govern.
     The `http` suite became two when 1427-e split the module. The
     data-language suites are cx-core-data's and are read from its pinned
     checkout, spelled from conformance/ (RULED: RS-7, RS-9, RS-12): the docs
     a build embeds cite exactly the corpus of the module version it compiles. ]
  [suites [#
code
../deps/cx-core-data/conformance/core
llm/antipatterns
../deps/cx-core-data/conformance/operator_heads
platform/store
platform/journal
platform/http
stdlib/http-client
deps/cx-platform-agent/conformance/x/mcp
deps/cx-platform-agent/conformance/x/a2a
stdlib/strings
stdlib/fp
stdlib/json
stdlib/map
stdlib/array
stdlib/re
stdlib/time
stdlib/io
stdlib/env
stdlib/cx
stdlib/format
stdlib/authz
platform/authz-store
platform/session
stdlib/log
deps/cx-platform-ux/conformance/xap/ux
deps/cx-platform-ux/conformance/xap/ux-web
xap/xap-compose
xap/xap-dist
../deps/cx-core-data/conformance/xml
../deps/cx-core-data/conformance/table
../deps/cx-core-data/conformance/identity
stdlib/validate
#]]

  [output id=primer out='primer.md' template='primer.md.tmpl'
          section=start
          title='CX primer for LLM assistants'
    [summary [#
The one file to load before writing CX. The surface taught through runnable
examples, a ring decision table, the core idioms, and the anti-patterns that
Lisp/Clojure/shell priors produce — each with its real diagnostic.
#]]]

  [output id=data out='reference-data-language.md'
          template='reference-data-language.md.tmpl'
          title='Reference: the CX data language'
    [summary [#
Elements, attributes, scalars and their auto-typing, collections, raw text,
tables, identity anchors, and the format conversions the data reading offers.
#]]]

  [output id=code out='reference-code-language.md'
          template='reference-code-language.md.tmpl'
          title='Reference: the CX code language'
    [summary [#
The directive surface — binding, control flow, comprehensions, pattern
matching, definitions, pipes, CXPath — and how errors propagate as values.
#]]]

  [output id=stdlib out='reference-stdlib.md'
          template='reference-stdlib.md.tmpl'
          title='Reference: the standard library'
    [summary [#
The bundled module catalog, the [?lib] import form, and the capability each
effectful pack requires.
#]]]

  [output id=cli out='reference-cli.md' template='reference-cli.md.tmpl'
          title='Reference: the cx command line'
    [summary [#
The run surface, the convert surface, the subcommand catalog, and the
capability grant flags — including the flag-placement rule that silently
denies capabilities when broken.
#]]]

  [output id=playbook-xap out='playbook-xap.md'
          template='playbook-xap.md.tmpl'
          section=build
          title='Playbook: building a production XAP'
    [summary [#
The full arc for a production feature deployment: authoring and composing
feature grammars, derived nouns and deriver principals, the authority model,
the *.xap.cxd deployment document and its [runtime] bindings, offline identity
bootstrap, the deployment host, and the ux-web hypermedia surface — with what
the rulings forbid stated as plainly as what they require.
#]]]

  [output id=model-customization out='model-customization.md'
          template='model-customization.md.tmpl'
          section=build
          title='The customization model: what one tenant may change'
    [summary [#
The question an adopter asks first and the layer answers in seven places: what
a tenant may change, what must change for everyone, how a change reaches a
fleet on both the surface and the grammar plane, and why behaviour is not
tenant-customizable — the refusals stated as the promise they enforce rather
than as friction.
#]]]

  [output id=platform out='reference-platform.md'
          template='reference-platform.md.tmpl'
          title='Reference: the platform and ecosystem rings'
    [summary [#
The platform group (store, journal, HTTP, XAP host) and the bindings and
ecosystem groups (agent interop, feature distribution, language bindings) —
what is reachable and how.
#]]]
]
